# Spec-Driven Delivery

Start development with the `spec` skill (`/spec <intent>`); `/spec-next` orients in work already under way. Explicit
user instructions take precedence. Never end the user's session on your own.

## 1. Context

- Read this guide once per agent context before development work, including work started without a skill.
- Read each relevant instruction listed with it before the action it governs. Instructions hold judgment and policy,
  playbooks (`devkit-playbook`) occasional procedures and rules (`devkit rules`) deterministic checks.
- If an instruction conflicts with a mandatory check, follow the check and report the conflict.
- Add a rule or check only for an identifiable failure or a reusable practice, at proportionate cost.

## 2. Living Spec and Scope

- `docs/` is the Living Spec, the current contract (capabilities, stories, architecture, decisions): rewrite changed
  statements in place. History belongs in Git, ADRs and dated materials; temporary task lists in `specs/`.
- A feature spec is `specs/<YYYY-MM>/<YYYYMMDD>-<slug>/` (`spec.md`, `plan.md`, `tasks.md`) and stays as history once
  completed. Read current contracts and active specs (`devkit spec list spec`) before drafting new work.
- A package may keep a Living Spec namespace under `docs/packages/<name>/`: `list` and `search` cover the current
  directory's namespace plus global documents (`--all-namespaces` for cross-package impact); IDs are repository-wide.
- Refer to ID-bearing documents by full ID (`` `CAP-...` ``), never by path (`devkit spec resolve <ID>`,
  `devkit spec refs <ID> --direction out|in`).
- Implement no Non-Goal and no unrequested feature, refactor or speculative scope; an unstated assumption grants no
  permission. A lasting policy change or an exception to a MUST needs an ADR; state a one-off exception within the
  approved scope in the review or final report.
- A task's `_Boundary:_` is the expected scope: explain a necessary extension in the report. Write boundaries given to
  a delegated role are strict.

## 3. Classification and Workflows

Classify authorized work on three axes:

- **`task-kind`**: `feature` (default) or `bugfix` (a defect against an agreed contract: reproduce, Red then Green).
- **`planning-depth`**: what must be settled first, not size. `session`: nothing, no Unit. `spec`: the contract, with
  a clear direction; a spec Unit. `proposal`: the direction; a proposal Unit, Planning, then handoff.
- **`activity-effort`**: `low`, `medium` (default) or `high` per activity: how thorough review and verification are.
  It never lowers gates, exit checks or `required` policies.

The workflow is `sdd-<planning-depth>[-<task-kind>]`: `sdd-session-feature`, `sdd-session-bugfix`,
`sdd-spec-feature`, `sdd-spec-bugfix` and `sdd-proposal` (handed off to `sdd-spec-<task_kind>`); a team review is
`proposal` depth plus PR policy. `overall-refactor` (a structural change across the project, no new behavior) has no
depth: `sdd-overall-refactor`, `direct` only, no Unit.

- Only the Host raises the depth (`[tool.spec] depth_escalation`), never a worker. Only a proposal's handoff lowers it,
  or the session reclassification of a queued draft on the user's explicit decision at pickup.
- Unit depths write `spec.md`, `plan.md` and `tasks.md` before production code.
- Follow the workflow definition's stages, checks, gates and returns, never an ad-hoc sequence
  (`devkit workflow next --unit <dir>`).

Read `devkit spec policy` before classification, gate and Git decisions. The main decision boundaries are
`plan-accept` (proposal direction), `proposal-handoff` (start Development) and `dev-integration` (accept the
Development result); each is `ask` (default) or `auto`.

- `plan-intent` records goal, scope, verification, expected bugfix behavior, `task-kind` and `planning-depth`. An
  explicit task instruction supplies that intent: do not request the same consent again. When a Unit needs the
  record, write it with `devkit workflow answer --by user`; `auto` satisfies it by policy.
- Accept proposals with `devkit spec proposal accept --by user|policy`, using `policy` only under owning
  `plan-accept=auto`. Source-only auto never grants authority; prospective revocation stops remaining effects.
- A native accept-and-handoff form may cover both decisions in one explicit approval. Otherwise keep them separate,
  so accepted proposals can wait in Backlog.
- Within approved scope the Host chooses sequencing, parallel workers, implementation, review and fixes without extra
  user approvals; a scope or constraint change needs new authority.
- Records name `user` or `policy` and carry decisions, never conversation quotes, paraphrases or dates; a decline note
  states the reason as a decision. Report stages the policy switched off as skipped.
{% if execution.environment == "local" %}- Shared base `dev-integration=auto` authorizes verified local merge and exact cleanup through the MCP executor; it grants no publication authority or host permission.
{% else %}- Shared base `dev-integration=auto` grants no cloud mutation or publication authority. Return verified results to the Host unless a separately authorized CLI/PR route exists; host permissions still apply.
{% endif %}
Before formal worktree work, read the `spec` skill's `references/worktree.md`; before completion or Git actions, its
`references/completion.md`.

The Host chooses the mode per activity within authorized scope and resource constraints, with no approval solely for
delegation: `direct` (default; the Host implements) or `local-orchestrated` (one-shot workers in their own
worktrees). `cloud-pr` requires existing external execution and publication authority. A `session` task runs only in
`direct` or the `cloud-pr` general PR path.

## 4. Verification and Completion

- Before reporting completion, compare the final diff, the agreed requirements, the check results and the Living
  Spec impact (`spec-review`, `spec-sync`).
- Fix implementation defects rather than rewriting documentation to endorse them.
- Reconcile changed behavior, capabilities and constraints into the Living Spec; recheck after further changes.
- Spec Units keep a resolved `reconcile.md` and stay in place when completed.
- A `session` task writes no Unit, envelope, gate record, planning or reconcile file. It reports the change, effort,
  review result, verification and remaining constraints in its final response or PR body; a needed Living Spec change
  raises it to `spec`.
- The `rules-check` commit gate (on by default) runs the spec rules on every commit; `devkit rules check --staged`
  reproduces it. CI runs `devkit rules check --ci --base <ref>`.
- Completion, commit, integration and ending the session are separate events.

## 5. Templates and Policy Files

- Templates: `.devkit/managed/spec/templates/{capability,story,decision,proposal,milestone}.md`.
- Policy lives in `.devkit/config.toml` (`[workflow.approvals]`, `[workflow.stages]`, `[worktree]`, `[tool.spec]`);
  read it through `devkit spec policy`, never by parsing the file.
- Change `.devkit/managed/` through `devkit override` or the project's own bundle, never by editing it.
